00:14:42.520 --> 00:15:02.520
If you know Jewish geography, Aleppo was a key city between the caravans coming from the north, Iran and Iraq. And when they would go down south to Egypt or to Israel, they had to pass through Aleppo.

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:28.520
And Aleppo had one great advantage. It's a seaport. And there he goes, Sadia el-Fayoumi. And he remains for a while. We don't know what happened and how he developed. But at the time, he was able to write a poem. And that poem tells you a little bit about his mind.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:51.520
He writes it in Hebrew. It happened to be that when he was in Aleppo, he went to a tailor. And very, you know, in a joking way, he said, I have to ask the tailor, because the tailor was taking him a long time to finish his suit. He says, how many stitches do you make in my suit that takes you so long?

00:15:51.520 --> 00:16:18.520
And he says to him, why do you ask me how many stitches? Let me ask you how many letters there are in the Tora. He said, I don't have to think. For a while, he composed on the spot a poem. This poem is very well written. It tells you how many times the aleph appears, how many times the bet appears, how many times the giman appears.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:37.520
Every single letter. And not only the first letters, but also the first and the last letters, like kap and ghaf, because it's a kap at the beginning and a ghaf at the end. And nun and a nun sofi. And mem and a mem. And he composes on the spot a poem. We have it today.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:55.520
Yes? By the way, I happened to write down the first aleph, how many aleph there are, something like 42,000 and whatever it is, and a few hundred. Yes? Exactly, exactly how many. And this just tells you a little bit about the mind of Zechariah Gaon. What?

00:16:55.520 --> 00:17:20.520
A computer. A computer cannot do it, because you have to program the computer. More than a computer. On the spot. It took him a few seconds. He had to think, because he took it off guard, that tailor. And he had to think, and he said, and then he composed it. On the spot, he makes the poem, in which it's called Shir HaOtiot. It's a famous poem.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:38.520
And by the way, it has been checked and rechecked by modern authorities, and it's absolutely perfect. Now, something happened, and now he begins to act in history.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:18:03.520
He, when he was going to Aleppo, obviously he had spent some time in Israel. And in Israel, there were schools. The truth is that they were not of the same category as the schools in Babylon. There was a man by the name of Aharon ben Meir.

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:28.520
He was the head of the school in Tiberias. And he figures out when the new month should be. And according to him, Pesah was going to fall on Tuesday. Now, in Babylon, they figured out that Pesah should fall not on Tuesday, but on Thursday.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:48.520
Now, there was for the first time in Jewish history a two-day difference in the calendar. Now, this man, ben Meir, he argued that Israel and not Babylonia should be the center of Jewish authority.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:19:08.520
And therefore, he goes to Harazetim, Mount Aleph, and he publicly declared that the new Pesah should fall on Tuesday and not on Thursday. The person who understood the importance of this new split was the Aharon.

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:30.520
The authorities in Babylon, they didn't know what to do. Nobody was able to prove in a mathematical, astronomical way who was right and who was wrong. And while the Aharon was in Aleppo, he was commissioned to handle this matter.

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:56.520
And he writes about the calendar. And he's able to prove in such a conclusive way that the man in Israel was wrong by two days. That after one year, the whole issue is settled. And in a way, he becomes recognized as one of the most important authorities of his time.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:20.520
He goes up north and he goes to Babylonia. To make the story short, after a very short time, something with no precedence occurs in Babylonia. He is appointed as the Rosh ha-Shana. Why there is no precedent? I want to tell you a little bit about Jews in Judaism.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:39.520
We always claim knowledge and so on. That's important. But historically, it was not always so. In Babylonia, for instance, they would never appoint somebody to the position of Rosh ha-Shana unless he belonged to the aristocratic families.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:21:05.520
Now, not only that Sehali Akaon was not an aristocrat, not only that his father did not belong to one of the important families, but he was not even Babylonian. And don't forget, according to the Christians, the first time, I think one of the first times that we have a non-Italian pope is today. The pope always was Italian. But moreover, in the Middle Ages, he always belonged to the aristocracy.

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:27.520
So, Sehali Akaon was an absolutely fascinating person because he was the first man to be appointed. The one who appointed him was the Res Galuta, the Jewish exilar, the prince. And his name was, and remember it correct, David Ben Zakkai.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:47.520
Now, when he wanted to appoint Sehali Akaon, he goes and he asks one of the old Hakhamim, he says, should I appoint Sehali Akaon? He says, no, don't appoint him. He says, why not? He says, because he's Hakham, he's a great scholar.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:22:11.520
He has no fear of men, and he is absolutely devoted to his religious duties. You know what that means? You cannot manipulate him. Don't appoint him. But David Ben Zakkai wanted so much to have a great Hakham, so to rebuild Judaism, that he appointed him.

00:22:12.520 --> 00:22:35.520
And something happened a few months later. A few months later, somebody dies, and he leaves an enormous inheritance into the millions and millions of dollars. And Ben Zakkai decides that the inheritance belongs to Mr. X.

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:51.520
And he goes and sends, because the Ribbi has to sign the document, otherwise it's not accepted by the Muslim court as a valid thing. He writes the document, and he sends it to Sehali Akaon that he should sign it.

00:22:52.520 --> 00:23:05.520
Sehali Akaon very politely says that he's not going to sign it, because he thought that the inheritance did not belong to Mr. A, but to Mr. B, whatever it is. We don't know the exact details.

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:24.520
So David Ben Zakkai sends the document with the San Yehudah to Sehali Akaon, and we have exactly the words. He says, tell this Shotei to sign the papers.

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:41.520
Now, he has this fool to sign the papers. Now, the son knew that Sehali Akaon was a great man, and he did not call him this, but he went and begged him.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:55.520
And Sehali Akaon refuses, because we don't know the details, but he believes it's contrary to the law. To make the story short, the son goes back and forth 15 times.

00:23:55.520 --> 00:24:13.520
In the last time, the son loses his temper, and he says, you either sign it, or I'll hit you. He was a young man. And at that moment, hell broke loose for the first time in history.

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:30.520
They go in and say, Jews never had a sense of theater. And in one of my articles, I said, whoever lived in the Jewish community didn't need the theater.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:51.520
So anyhow, the Rosh Yishiva, Sehali Akaon, appoints a new exilarch. He says, this guy, if he threatens the Hakhamim, is not worthy to become a Rosh Yishiva.

00:24:52.520 --> 00:25:13.520
And the Rosh Yishiva appoints a new Gaon. Now, I'm not going to go into the details, but if you want to write a nice play, a fantastic drama, with a lot of intervals of laughter, you can write about this historical event.

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:34.520
It lasted for 13 years. For 13 years, there was total non-speaking terms between the two most important figures of the Jews.

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:48.520
Meanwhile, the Karaim and the Muslims, they were having a great time, because they would point out how the Jews, yes, the so-called representative of tradition and so on, they cannot get along with each other.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:26:10.520
Eventually, they make reconciliation. Sehali Akaon dies at the age of 60, and the guy dies even before Sehali Akaon. But something very important happened in 13 years, since Sehali Akaon was officially could not function.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:26.520
He was producing students, and let me tell you what he did in those 13 years. I once had a dream, and I remember the dream, that I was in the old house where I was born.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:40.520
This is true. I had a dream like this. And we used to, you know, like in the old times, to get together, and there was no television those times. And, you know, like the grandfather and so on, and used to tell stories.

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:55.520
And somebody in my dream says, tell us a story about Sehali Akaon. And I said, you know, you see this room? If we could put all the books of Sehali Akaon, it would fill two-thirds of all this room.

00:26:55.520 --> 00:27:16.520
He contributed in the following areas. First of all, during that time, Hebrew was almost never studied. The Bible was completely inaccessible to the people.

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:39.520
The first he did is a translation of the Bible, the entire Bible in Arabic. And that translation is not a literal translation, but it is an interpretation. If any of you knows Arabic, you know what the word sharah means, Mr. Sasson? What does the word sharah mean?

00:27:40.520 --> 00:28:00.520
Interpretation. Interpretation. Does not mean translation. Sharah means actually interpretation. And it is a translation which at the same time, yes, interprets the Bible.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:17.520
Now, what he did with this was that the whole Bible became accessible to the Jews. And two things happened at the same time. Number one, the Jews before that, they had no opportunity to learn Arabic.

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:32.520
Because the Jews now, they learned the Bible in Arabic, and it was written in a very good, still with the sharah. You know, remember when you were children, you studied the sharah? It became a very kind of classy language.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:47.520
All of a sudden, the Jews had access through the Bible, because they knew the Bible inside out. They knew it by heart. So when we were children, we still studied by heart. So what happened?

00:28:47.520 --> 00:29:08.520
That the Jewish people became conversant in a very high class Arabic. Second, every Jew was able to understand for the first time the Bible, the source of Judaism, became accessible to the public.

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:28.520
But he did something more with the Bible. He started writing commentaries, notes on the Bible. And when he did something so beautiful, he wrote introductions to the entire Bible and to every single book.

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:55.520
He tells you who wrote the book, at what time it was written, what are the major points of the book, what are the ideas to be developed, what did we learn from the book. So in other words, you open the book of Shaddaa Gaon, you read the introduction, you understand what the book is doing about, then you read the sharah, which is the translation, and then it has notes in all the difficult words.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:30:10.520
And he tells you why he translated it this way, why he translated it the other way. And all of a sudden, the Jewish people become unlearned people. Again, this was not present then.

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:34.520
The first time that the Bible is translated into a colloquial language, into spoken language, it was by Shaddaa Gaon. That's the first thing that he did. Also, another thing that he did through this translation is that the intellectual language of the Jew before Arabic, do you know what it was?

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:55.520
Aramaic. Aramaic was a dead language at the time. There was no possibility of doing Aramaic. It changed from Aramaic, all of a sudden Arabic. And by having been Arabic, a Jew becomes a member of the new culture, has access to the culture.

00:30:55.520 --> 00:31:19.520
He composed the first dictionary. He writes Haigron. Haigron is the first dictionary ever written of the Hebrew language. It was written by Shaddaa Gaon. And he does two things in the dictionary that are almost amazing.

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:38.520
Number one, he writes a dictionary. In the dictionary, he has a dictionary of rhymes. If you want to rhyme one word with another, so that you want to write poems, poetry, you want to write, I don't know, something that rhymes with the word bite.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:57.520
He has all the rhymes, every single word, how one word rhymes with the other. By doing this, you know what he did? He opened the door for the people to write poetry.

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:21.520
And finally, in the same dictionary, he has the major rules to understand how verbs, how nouns are inflected in Hebrew, so that if a person takes this dictionary, not only understands the words, he could write and he could understand how to write. He could become a poet.

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:45.520
Another book that he wrote, he was very much concerned against the Karaites. He knew that the Karaites were very tempting because they were criticizing the Jews. And unfortunately, many of the criticism was valid. So what does he do? He writes a book called Seventy Words.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:33:04.520
And what does he do? He finds that there are seventy words in the Bible. Now, if they occur only once in the Bible, what is the meaning of the word? If it occurs more than once, you could know through the context and so on what the word, you know, for example, if you say,

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:14.520
But if you have the word, I don't know,

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:32.520
It occurs only once in the Bible. How do you know what the word means? Now, and every one of those seventy words that occur only once in the Bible, he gives you the meaning. And it's the correct meaning. But he shows you how? Through tradition.

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:54.520
And therefore, he says, without tradition, the Bible cannot be read. You cannot understand Hebrew just by reading Hebrew. You need a living tradition. And therefore, he takes seventy words, and with these seventy words, he shows that you cannot read the Bible without a tradition.

00:33:54.520 --> 00:34:06.520
Then he writes another book, and it's called The Book of Hebrew for those who understand the Bible, what the word means, how the Bible develops. In other words, the literary aspect of the Bible.

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:26.520
Now, besides writing on the Bible, as he did, he wrote on halachah, on Jewish law. If you read the Talmud, as I do it every day, I tell you, there is no one thing for sure, that the Talmud never develops something systematically.

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:43.520
It's very, very curt, very short, very brief, very elliptic. What's the other one he did? He wrote monographs. He takes a subject, say inheritance. He develops a whole concept of inheritance. There are different points.

00:34:43.520 --> 00:35:01.520
And after he has the idea in a totality, he brings you all the cases, so that they are arranged in perfect order, and you understand why the principles are the way they are. And not only he wrote it systematically, but again he writes it in Arabic.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:20.520
Why is it so important? Arabic was the English of the time. And he writes on every aspect pertaining to family laws. Marriage, divorce, widow, the rights of the widow, the rights of a woman who is left without children.

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:39.520
And all those types of things. By the way, just to give you an example. You are from Egypt, so you know this. About 60 years ago, the brother of Hakham Aharon Ben Shim'on, you know what he did? Hakham Aharon Ben Shim'on was the chief Ribbi of Egypt.

00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:56.520
His brother, he took the book of Sihadi Aharon, An Inheritance, and he put it in Arabic letters, because the Jews wrote it with Hebrew letters, and he presented it to the government, and the government accepted it as the official code of the laws of inheritance of the Jewish people.

00:35:56.520 --> 00:36:20.520
Imagine a book written over a thousand years ago could serve in the modern society of Egypt 56 years ago as a modern code. Not superseded from a thousand years ago. Not superseded. Besides writing, and therefore what happened? All of a sudden the Jew has access to Tora and the state of Havahar.

00:36:20.520 --> 00:36:41.520
But he did something, and this is what I'm going to do today. Oh, another thing. He wrote the first Siddur. He writes the Salaam, the phrase, in those days. He has a Hazzan. The Hazzan of the phrase. The phrase was written nowhere. You know it by heart, or you don't know it.

00:36:41.520 --> 00:37:01.520
And nobody understood, except the few geniuses in Babylonia, nobody understood not only the meaning, but the function. The first Siddur ever written, it was written by Sihadi Aharon. It still is available. I have it.

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:30.520
And it's called Kitab Jamaat al-Salawat wal-Tasabih. Means a book containing Jamaat al-Salawat, means all the Salaat, wal-Tasabih means the songs. And he does the following. Not only he puts everything in order, he has an introduction to every part of the tefilla in Arabic, explaining you the meaning of the tefilla, the laws, and what are the buts and the ifs.

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:50.520
So now, if a Jew opens this book, at the time, he had direct access to all the prayers. By the way, among the many things that he invented, he invented something, a new kind of poetry. And you know how he called that new kind of poetry? The first person to ever invent this.

