Oh, that's right. That's right. I'll come to it in a second. But yeah, I'll come in a second. Imanat is something that you understand. You think about it and you believe about it. That is Imanat.

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:30.520
It's a faith that somehow could be expressed in rational terms. But the way it is in Arabic is something that you tie yourself to. A commitment. And there are two kinds. There is faith. You think about it. You study it.

00:40:30.520 --> 00:40:53.520
And you say, there are certain aspects in every religion, in every spiritual system that you can never prove. But you commit yourself. You tie yourself to it. And therefore, Judaism not only demands faith, but also commitments.

00:40:53.520 --> 00:41:17.520
No, no, no. It's not a tradition. It's a commitment. It's something that you don't know. You cannot prove it. But you commit yourself to this. For instance, do we know that the Mashiah is going to come? We don't know that the Mashiah is going to come. But we have a commitment towards this. Do we know that there is going to be a Taqiyyat al-Madin? We don't know.

00:41:17.520 --> 00:41:37.520
But this is no longer a belief. I mean, can you prove it? No. This is a commitment. Do we know that there is resurrection after death? Or that there is immortality of the soul? That our soul doesn't die? No. This is a commitment. So it's very beautiful. It works very intelligently.

00:41:37.520 --> 00:42:03.520
It's amanat wa etikadat. It's not only emuna, but also commitment. So this is the first book in which he opened a new door for the Jews. Traditionally, if you want to know what faith is, you ask the Gaon in Babylonia. And he tells you from his chair, he declares what Judaism is all about.

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:24.520
Reason played very little role in Judaism. For the first time, the other Gaon says, no. Reason plays a very important role in Judaism. And Judaism can be studied and analyzed in rational terms.

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:47.520
And he writes this book. And this book is still today one of the great masterpieces of religious philosophy written by anyone. We're going to read a few pages, just for you to have an idea of the kind of a person he was. And I think it will be very interesting. This way you will know a little bit about Yair Yagar. My mother did a lot, but not this.

00:42:47.520 --> 00:43:09.520
The first one who did this. The first one. Rambam did translate the Mishna into Arabic. That was the first time. But the first one who wrote everything. You see, sometimes they ask me, who is greater, Yair Yagar or my mother? I believe that both of them were superb geniuses. I believe really that my mother was greater.

00:43:09.520 --> 00:43:36.520
But in honesty, if there would not have been a Yair Yagar on 250 years before Rambam, Rambam would never have been born. After there was a Yair Yagar on, then knowledge came all about. People started writing poetry. People understood the Bible. People started criticizing. By the way, the first grammarian, he is the first grammarian. People, they never studied grammar before. The Bible was a gospel.

00:43:36.520 --> 00:44:03.520
Now, after, for instance, today we read Ibn 'Ezra. And you say, oh, how brilliant Ibn 'Ezra is. Eighty percent of what Ibn 'Ezra is, is a reaction in favor or against what Yair Yagar on wrote. If it would not have been Yair Yagar on, there would not have been no Ibn 'Ezra. Or we say, oh, how beautiful Yehuda ha-Levi as a poet is. But if it would not have been because of the Bakashot written by Yair Yagar on, there would not have been no Yehuda ha-Levi.

00:44:04.520 --> 00:44:12.520
So, in a sense, he is the pioneer who began with a new sense of what Judaism is all about, Mr. Sutton.

